            <div class="imagemeta wide col-md-7">
                <h3>Summary</h3>
                <p>I joined Trupanion as the <strong>first design hire</strong> on the B2B side of the company. Their flagship product, a SaaS dashboard for filing veterinary insurance claims, was a major source of new clients, but had been developed largely by executives standing over developers' shoulders or filing tickets without formal user research or documentation.</p>
                <p>In my time at Trupanion, I established a <strong>research-backed iterative design practice,</strong> component libraries, design QA, and a workflow for collaboration between stakeholders, designers and developers.</p>
                <p>Beginning as a contractor, I was later promoted to <strong>FTE B2B design lead</strong> and grew the team to include two additional designers as we launched and iterated on the hospital software's redesign and grew our capacity to multiple simultaneous B2B projects.</p>
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="imagemeta col-md-6">
                <h3>Design Challenge</h3>


                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Increase lead generation</strong> from a dashboard suffering from extreme information overload. </li>
                    <li>Increase <strong>completion rate and reduce errors</strong> on a large complex webform being used in a hurry by people in cognitively overwhelming environments. </li>
                    <li>Evangelize <strong>consistency, user testing practices and component-based frontend development</strong> to a team accustomed to building everything as a one-off.</li>
                </ul>
                </div>

  
            <div class="images col-md-6">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/trupanion/summary_min.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="images col-md-5">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/trupanion/initial_research.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta wide col-md-7">
                <h3>Establishing a research practice</h3>
                <p>My first order of business at Trupanion was to map the existing software and begin to document an understanding of our software's life in the wild that had previously only existed as fragmented tribal knowledge. This included:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Site visits with the sales staff to interview and observe the users <strong>actually using our product.</strong> (Previously our feedback came mainly from administrators who made purchase decisions but didn't themselves use the software.)</li>
                    <li><strong>Regular workshops with my team, stakeholders and teams from the rest of the company</strong> to socialize findings, set expectations, and evangelize our methodology.</li>
                    <li>Interviews with tech support and customer service teams. In those interviews, I heard something I've heard at a number of companies before and since:</li> 
                </ul>
                                    <p class="pquote">"No one's ever asked us about these things before, we've been hoping someone would."</p>

            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>


<section class="projectsection ">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  

            <div class="imagemeta wide col-md-6">
                <h3>Bite sized pieces</h3>
                <p>From the beginning, I was able to identify frontend inconsistencies that had resulted from the app being built piecemeal. I set about creating a pattern library and set of spec docs to unify our UI elements and began a design QA process to ensure things were built to spec.</p>
               
                <p>During this time I also developed the system I still use today of multiple Figma files that serve as a set of specs for developers, a showroom for stakeholders, and a factory floor for the design team. Each entry is coded with metadata similar to Git commit messages that let anyone see where in the process a set of screens was created, what their status is, and what changed from previous entries. I've been asked to implement this same workflow by every other team I've demoed the process to.</p>

            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-6">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/trupanion/components_2.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">

            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/trupanion/research_lockup.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta col-md-5 col-md-offset-1">
                <h3>Unifying and operationalizing</h3>
                <p>As the project progressed, I was given resources to <strong>build out the team</strong> with additional research and design staff. I moved to focusing on design ops, strategy, mentoring and doing design QA while my team ran tests, iterated on our designs and gained valuable insights on the new ideas.</p>
            </div>
  

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>


<section class="projectsection">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="imagemeta center col-md-10 col-md-offset-1">
                <h3>Results: A portal that performs</h3>
                <p>After launch, our new portal was able to deliver <strong>solid improvements</strong> on the previous versions including:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>16.9% dashboard lead growth</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>~30% increase in users following golden path claim submission process</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>13% B2B customer increase after 6 months</strong></li> 
                    <li><strong>24% B2C customer increase after 6 months</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

